  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA warm front will lift into the region on Saturday, allowing\nshowers to spread east into the region throughout the day. There\nmay be just enough destabilization for some embedded\nthunderstorms during the afternoon and early evening, although\nwidespread cloud cover and rain may prevent destabilization that\nwould be sufficient for stronger storms. As of now, the best\ninstability will be to the east and southeast of the area,\nalthough a few stronger storms can't be ruled out, especially if\nshower/cloud coverage ends up being lower than expected in\neastern zones during peak diurnal instability Saturday\nafternoon. There will likely be a break in showers/storms\nSaturday night before scattered showers and possibly a few\nthunderstorms potentially develop as a cold front moves \nsoutheast across the area at some point on Sunday. There will \ndefinitely be better forcing/shear on Sunday afternoon which may\nsupport some organized showers/storms, although there is still \nquite a bit of uncertainty in cloud cover, moisture, \ninstability, and the exact timing of the cold front. SPC has\nplaced locations generally east of I-75 in a Marginal Risk\n(level 1 of 5) of severe weather Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nDry weather will return to the region for Monday, however\nunsettled weather with periods of showers and storms will return \nto the region Tuesday night through late week as a series of \ndisturbances move across the area. A warm and humid air mass \nwith daytime temperatures in the 80s and dew points in the 60s \nand lower 70s will be in place over the region, which may allow \nfor the potential for stronger storms and maybe heavy rainfall. \nThat said, it's still a bit too far out to have high confidence \nin the timing/placement of storms and any associated hazards.",
